@@ -0,0 +1,863 @@
+//! Issue #771 — the multipart **retirement obligation**: the `retire:bytes:<token>` /
+//! `retire:records:<token>` *value* (`wyrd_core::multipart::{RetirePayload, PartScope,
+//! PartNumberSet, RetireGeneration, decode_retire_obligation}`), proposal 0016 §1 (`:346-356`),
+//! its token grammar (`:358-388`), its decode boundary (`:390-441`) and the writer table the
+//! shapes are derived from (`:659-673`, `:2187`, `:2193`, `:2417`).
+//!
+//! **Pure**: no store, no async, no fixture beyond hand-authored JSON bytes and the production
+//! codec — mirroring `multipart_session_records.rs` (#716) for the record value this child adds
+//! beside it.
+//!
+//! Every witness is **decoded, never constructed**: [`RetirePayload`] has no writer-side
+//! constructor (the first writers are the store round trips, #656–#659), exactly as the session
+//! and part records have none. Bytes are written out by hand and fed to two surfaces: the
+//! module's own attributed, **key-taking** `decode_retire_obligation` (S2) and the store-wide
+//! `metadata::decode::<RetirePayload>` (S1). The two cannot agree about everything, and the
+//! disagreement is the point of this record class: S1 cannot see the key, so the four
+//! key-relation rules (mode, token scope, generation identity, segment epoch) are S2's alone.
+//! The `decode_both` helper below therefore asserts the containment that *is* meaningful — S2's
+//! accepted set is inside S1's and agrees with it on the value — plus the value-level rules both
+//! seams must refuse alike.
+//!
+//! **Serialization identity is asserted on every accepted witness, not test by test.**
+//! `decode_both` re-encodes whatever it accepted and compares it byte-for-byte against the input,
+//! so `encode(decode(bytes)) == bytes` is a property of the *whole* file's accepted set. Every
+//! retirement obligation is installed under `require_absent(retire:<mode>:<token>)` and drained
+//! under `require(retire:… == prior)` (`0016:369-373`, `:667`), so a re-encode that is not the
+//! identity is a record nothing can precondition on (`AGENTS.md:170-172`).
+//!
+//! The legs (Falsifiability, brief #771): **nine** isolating negations — R2 (an obligation owing
+//! nothing), R3 (mode-in-the-key), R4 in both directions (a session-wide component under a
+//! per-part token; the per-part component under a session-wide one), R5 (generation identity),
+//! R6 (the canonical token epoch), R7 (nested chunk geometry), R8 (the part-number set's
+//! canonical spelling) and R9 (the canonical-bytes gate) — each asserted in **one** test, so
+//! removing one production check fails exactly one test. R1's completeness legs are the other
+//! way round: the eight writer shapes each decode under their own key, and a payload type whose
+//! arms made `{session, parts}` or `{parts} + {seg}` inexpressible would fail those.
+//!
+//! Two boundary witnesses pin what this child deliberately does **not** check: a `ChunkRef` whose
+//! `placement` length disagrees with its scheme still decodes (the standing contextual check,
+//! liberal on read — ADR-0045 `:45-49`, `:72`; `AGENTS.md:146-149`; `0016:416-432`), and a
+//! generation's segment group carries an epoch no token can be checked against.
+//!
+//! No `#![cfg(...)]` here — this file always compiles and always runs.
+
+#![forbid(unsafe_code)]
+
+use wyrd_core::metadata::{self, EcScheme, SegmentGroup};
+use wyrd_core::multipart::{
+    decode_retire_obligation, retire_key, AttemptId, PartNumber, PartNumberSet, PartScope,
+    RecordError, RetireMode, RetirePayload, RetireToken, UploadId, MAX_PART_NUMBER,
+};
+
+/// The session every session-scoped witness belongs to, and the fence epoch its token names.
+/// The epoch is deliberately neither `0` nor `1`: a witness that only ever observes a field's
+/// zero value cannot tell a decoder that reads it from one that returns a default, and R6's
+/// `E ± 1` legs need room on both sides.
+const EPOCH: u64 = 7;
+/// The generation a `g:` token names — neither component zero, for the same reason.
+const INODE: u64 = 42;
+const VERSION: u64 = 4;
+/// The part attempt a per-part token names.
+const PART: u32 = 3;
+
+/// 32 lowercase-hex characters from a 2-character pair — the length an upload id, an attempt id
+/// and a segment-group nonce all share (`0016:493-497`).
+fn hex32(pair: &str) -> String {
+    pair.repeat(16)
+}
+
+fn upload_id() -> UploadId {
+    UploadId::new(hex32("a1")).expect("32 lowercase-hex characters are an upload id")
+}
+
+/// The **suffix-free** session token — the whole-session obligations a fence, a publication or a
+/// rollback installs (`0016:358-366`).
+fn session_token() -> RetireToken {
+    RetireToken::Session {
+        upload_id: upload_id(),
+        epoch: EPOCH,
+        part: None,
+    }
+}
+
+/// The **per-part** session token — its optional `:<part-number>:<attempt-id>` suffix exists for
+/// exactly the per-part obligations (`0016:659`, `:672`, `:1620`).
+fn part_token() -> RetireToken {
+    RetireToken::Session {
+        upload_id: upload_id(),
+        epoch: EPOCH,
+        part: Some((
+            PartNumber::new(PART).expect("a part number in range"),
+            AttemptId::new(hex32("b2")).expect("32 lowercase-hex characters are an attempt id"),
+        )),
+    }
+}
+
+/// The generation token — `g:<inode-id>:<version>`, minted by exactly one publication.
+fn generation_token() -> RetireToken {
+    RetireToken::Generation {
+        inode: INODE,
+        version: VERSION,
+    }
+}
+
+/// A segment group's stored spelling, `{nonce, epoch}` (`crate::metadata::SegmentGroup`).
+fn seg_json(epoch: u64) -> String {
+    format!("{{\"nonce\":\"{}\",\"epoch\":{epoch}}}", hex32("c3"))
+}
+
+fn seg_group(epoch: u64) -> SegmentGroup {
+    SegmentGroup::new(hex32("c3"), epoch).expect("32 lowercase-hex characters are a nonce")
+}
+
+/// One chunk of an obligation's chunk list, spelled exactly as
+/// `wyrd_core::multipart`'s closed `ChunkRefWire` declares it: `id`, `scheme`, `len`,
+/// `placement`, in that order, none omitted.
+fn chunk_json(id: u128, scheme_json: &str, len: u64, placement: &str) -> String {
+    format!("{{\"id\":{id},\"scheme\":{scheme_json},\"len\":{len},\"placement\":{placement}}}")
+}
+
+/// A supported Reed-Solomon geometry — `erasure::supported(2, 1)`.
+const RS_2_1: &str = r#"{"ReedSolomon":{"k":2,"m":1}}"#;
+
+/// The chunk every `chunks` witness carries unless the leg perturbs it. Each field is
+/// deliberately non-default: a non-zero id, a real scheme, a non-zero length, a non-empty
+/// placement.
+fn chunk() -> String {
+    chunk_json(9, RS_2_1, 100, "[5,6,7]")
+}
+
+// ===========================================================================
+// S1/S2 containment + serialization identity — decode through the module's attributed,
+// key-taking `decode_retire_obligation` (S2) and the store-wide `metadata::decode` (S1), assert
+// what the two can agree on, and assert that re-encoding an accepted value reproduces the bytes
+// it was read from. Mirrors `decode_both` in `multipart_session_records.rs:169`, with the
+// asymmetry this record class introduces: S1 never sees the key.
+// ===========================================================================
+
+/// Decode `value` under `key` through S2, and the same bytes through S1, then assert:
+///
+/// * **containment + agreement** — everything S2 accepts, S1 accepts as the same payload (S2 is
+///   S1 plus the key relations and the canonical-bytes gate, never a different value);
+/// * **identity** — re-encoding an accepted payload reproduces the bytes it was read from, for
+///   *every* accepted witness in this file (R9);
+/// * **value-level symmetry** — a rejection that names a rule of the value alone is a rejection
+///   S1 must make too, so no value-level rule is quietly S2-only.
+///
+/// A key-relation rejection makes no claim on S1: it cannot see the key, which is exactly why
+/// `decode_retire_obligation` takes one.
+fn decode_both(key: &[u8], value: &[u8]) -> Result<(RetireToken, RetirePayload), RecordError> {
+    let typed = decode_retire_obligation(key, value);
+    let untyped = metadata::decode::<RetirePayload>(value);
+    match &typed {
+        Ok((_, payload)) => {
+            assert_eq!(
+                untyped.as_ref().ok(),
+                Some(payload),
+                "S1 and S2 disagree on {}",
+                String::from_utf8_lossy(value)
+            );
+            assert_eq!(
+                String::from_utf8_lossy(metadata::encode(payload).as_ref()),
+                String::from_utf8_lossy(value),
+                "decode->encode is not byte-identical for {payload:?}"
+            );
+        }
+        Err(err) if is_value_level(err) => assert!(
+            untyped.is_err(),
+            "S1 accepted {} that S2 refused on a value-level rule: {err:?}",
+            String::from_utf8_lossy(value)
+        ),
+        Err(_) => {}
+    }
+    typed
+}
+
+/// Whether a rejection names a rule of the **value alone** — one S1 must make as well. The
+/// key-relation variants (mode, token scope, token suffix, generation identity, segment epoch)
+/// and the canonical-bytes gate are deliberately absent: the first five need the key and the
+/// last needs the bytes, and S1 holds neither.
+fn is_value_level(err: &RecordError) -> bool {
+    matches!(
+        err,
+        RecordError::MalformedRecordValue { .. }
+            | RecordError::RetireObligationOwesNothing { .. }
+            | RecordError::ChunkSchemeUnsupported { .. }
+            | RecordError::PartNumberOutOfRange { .. }
+            | RecordError::PartNumberRunReversed { .. }
+            | RecordError::PartNumberRunsNotCoalesced { .. }
+    )
+}
+
+/// Assert that a decode was refused as this record class's attributed
+/// [`RecordError::MalformedRecordValue`], with the decoder's own message naming `needle`.
+fn assert_malformed<T: std::fmt::Debug>(result: Result<T, RecordError>, needle: &str) {
+    match result {
+        Err(RecordError::MalformedRecordValue { namespace, detail }) => {
+            assert_eq!(namespace, "retire:");
+            assert!(
+                detail.contains(needle),
+                "expected the decoder's message to name {needle:?}: {detail}"
+            );
+        }
+        other => panic!("expected MalformedRecordValue(retire:), got {other:?}"),
+    }
+}
+
+/// The runs a decoded [`PartScope::Set`] carries, or a panic naming what was found instead.
+fn runs(scope: Option<&PartScope>) -> Vec<(u32, u32)> {
+    match scope {
+        Some(PartScope::Set(set)) => set.runs().to_vec(),
+        other => panic!("expected an explicit part set, got {other:?}"),
+    }
+}
+
+// ===========================================================================
+// R1 — shape completeness. Every obligation `0016`'s writer rows install decodes from its own
+// key, as a SINGLE value under a SINGLE key. The combined shapes (`{session, parts}` and
+// `{parts} + {seg}`) are the reviewed defect of the archived attempts: a payload whose arms are
+// mutually exclusive forces a writer to install two records where the protocol installs one —
+// two keys where `require_absent` and the session's own emptiness gate expect one
+// (`0016:369-380`).
+// ===========================================================================
+
+/// `retire:bytes:` + a suffix-free `s:` token, `{session, all}` — the `Open` reap/abort fence
+/// (`0016:2187`, `:664`). `all` is the instruction to enumerate the session's own bounded
+/// `part:<id>:` range at drain time, which the fence has just made immutable.
+#[test]
+fn bytes_session_all_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    let value = br#"{"session":true,"parts":"all"}"#;
+    let (token, payload) = decode_both(&key, value).expect("the session teardown obligation");
+    assert_eq!(token, session_token());
+    assert!(payload.session());
+    assert_eq!(payload.parts(), Some(&PartScope::All));
+    assert!(payload.chunks().is_empty());
+    assert!(payload.generation().is_none());
+    assert!(payload.segments().is_none());
+}
+
+/// `retire:bytes:` + a suffix-free `s:` token, `{session, parts: <set>}` — the
+/// `Completing`→`Aborting` fence and the restore fence (`0016:665`, `:823`, `:2193`). **The
+/// combined shape**: one value carrying both the session's own records and the exact part set
+/// the fence froze.
+#[test]
+fn bytes_session_and_parts_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    let value = br#"{"session":true,"parts":[[1,4],[7,9]]}"#;
+    let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, value).expect("a session teardown naming its parts");
+    assert!(payload.session());
+    assert_eq!(runs(payload.parts()), vec![(1, 4), (7, 9)]);
+}
+
+/// `retire:bytes:` + a suffix-free `s:` token, `{parts: <set>}` alone — the root flip's
+/// **unnamed** staged parts, whose bytes are orphan-marked and whose records are then deleted by
+/// this one obligation (`0016:662`, `:919-921`). No `session` component: the session itself
+/// survives the publication.
+#[test]
+fn bytes_parts_alone_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    let value = br#"{"parts":[[2,2]]}"#;
+    let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, value).expect("the unnamed staged parts");
+    assert!(!payload.session());
+    assert_eq!(runs(payload.parts()), vec![(2, 2)]);
+}
+
+/// `retire:bytes:` + a suffix-free `s:` token, `{session}` alone — the abort/reap fence row's
+/// own spelling (`0016:664`), distinct from `{session, all}`: it owes the session's records and
+/// no part.
+#[test]
+fn bytes_session_alone_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    let value = br#"{"session":true}"#;
+    let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, value).expect("the session's own records");
+    assert!(payload.session());
+    assert!(payload.parts().is_none());
+}
+
+/// `retire:bytes:` + a **per-part** `s:` token, `{chunks: […]}` — a losing writer's
+/// compensation, a re-upload's superseded chunks, a post-staging local refusal (`0016:659`,
+/// `:672`, `:1620`). Its chunks have no naming record left, so the obligation carries them.
+#[test]
+fn bytes_chunks_under_a_per_part_token_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &part_token());
+    let value = format!("{{\"chunks\":[{}]}}", chunk()).into_bytes();
+    let (token, payload) = decode_both(&key, &value).expect("a per-part chunk obligation");
+    assert_eq!(token, part_token());
+    assert_eq!(payload.chunks().len(), 1);
+    assert_eq!(payload.chunks()[0].id, 9);
+    assert_eq!(
+        payload.chunks()[0].scheme,
+        EcScheme::ReedSolomon { k: 2, m: 1 }
+    );
+    assert_eq!(payload.chunks()[0].len, 100);
+    assert_eq!(payload.chunks()[0].placement, vec![5, 6, 7]);
+}
+
+/// `retire:bytes:` + a `g:` token, `{generation: {inode, version, chunks}}` — a superseded or
+/// deleted **flat-map** generation (`0016:355`, `:668`).
+#[test]
+fn bytes_generation_with_a_flat_map_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &generation_token());
+    let value = format!(
+        "{{\"generation\":{{\"inode\":{INODE},\"version\":{VERSION},\"chunks\":[{}]}}}}",
+        chunk()
+    )
+    .into_bytes();
+    let (token, payload) = decode_both(&key, &value).expect("a superseded flat generation");
+    assert_eq!(token, generation_token());
+    let generation = payload.generation().expect("a generation component");
+    assert_eq!(generation.inode(), INODE);
+    assert_eq!(generation.version(), VERSION);
+    assert_eq!(generation.chunks().len(), 1);
+    assert!(generation.segments().is_none());
+}
+
+/// `retire:bytes:` + a `g:` token, `{generation: {inode, version, segments}}` — a superseded or
+/// deleted **segmented** generation, named by its `seg:` key range rather than by frozen
+/// placements (`0016:2417-2425`).
+#[test]
+fn bytes_generation_with_segments_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &generation_token());
+    let value = format!(
+        "{{\"generation\":{{\"inode\":{INODE},\"version\":{VERSION},\"segments\":{}}}}}",
+        seg_json(2)
+    )
+    .into_bytes();
+    let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, &value).expect("a superseded segmented generation");
+    let generation = payload.generation().expect("a generation component");
+    assert!(generation.chunks().is_empty());
+    assert_eq!(generation.segments(), Some(&seg_group(2)));
+}
+
+/// `retire:records:` + a suffix-free `s:` token, `{parts: <set>}` — the root flip's **published**
+/// parts, whose bytes the published inode now protects (`0016:662`, `:919-921`).
+#[test]
+fn records_parts_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &session_token());
+    let value = br#"{"parts":[[1,4]]}"#;
+    let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, value).expect("the published parts' records");
+    assert_eq!(runs(payload.parts()), vec![(1, 4)]);
+}
+
+/// `retire:records:` + a suffix-free `s:` token, `{seg: {nonce, epoch}}` — one rolled-back
+/// `Completing` attempt's dangling segment records, naming **exactly that epoch's** keys
+/// (`0016:663`, `:665`, `:2357-2362`).
+#[test]
+fn records_seg_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &session_token());
+    let value = format!("{{\"seg\":{}}}", seg_json(EPOCH)).into_bytes();
+    let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, &value).expect("a rolled-back attempt's segments");
+    assert_eq!(payload.segments(), Some(&seg_group(EPOCH)));
+}
+
+/// `retire:records:` + a suffix-free `s:` token, `{parts: <set>, seg: {…}}` — **both in one
+/// payload** (`0016:356`, "and/or"). The second combined shape the archived attempts could not
+/// express.
+#[test]
+fn records_parts_and_seg_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &session_token());
+    let value = format!("{{\"parts\":[[1,4]],\"seg\":{}}}", seg_json(EPOCH)).into_bytes();
+    let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, &value).expect("parts and segments in one obligation");
+    assert_eq!(runs(payload.parts()), vec![(1, 4)]);
+    assert_eq!(payload.segments(), Some(&seg_group(EPOCH)));
+}
+
+// ===========================================================================
+// R2–R9 — the rejections. One test per rule, so removing one production check fails exactly one
+// test.
+// ===========================================================================
+
+/// **R2** — an obligation naming nothing is refused: residue nothing drains. A drain that met
+/// one would mark nothing, delete the obligation, and record the work as done.
+#[test]
+fn an_obligation_owing_nothing_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, b"{}"),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireObligationOwesNothing {
+            component: "payload"
+        })
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"chunks":[]}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireObligationOwesNothing {
+            component: "payload"
+        })
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":[]}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireObligationOwesNothing { component: "parts" })
+    );
+    let generation =
+        format!("{{\"generation\":{{\"inode\":{INODE},\"version\":{VERSION}}}}}").into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(
+            &retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &generation_token()),
+            &generation
+        ),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireObligationOwesNothing {
+            component: "generation"
+        })
+    );
+}
+
+/// **R3** — mode agreement. The mode lives in the key precisely so this is a decode error and
+/// never a misread boolean (`0016:434-441`): `chunks` and `generation` orphan-mark, so they are
+/// `retire:bytes:` only, and `seg` must never orphan anything (its segments' fragments are still
+/// protected by the `part:` records), so it is `retire:records:` only.
+#[test]
+fn a_component_under_the_wrong_mode_is_rejected() {
+    let chunks = format!("{{\"chunks\":[{}]}}", chunk()).into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &part_token()), &chunks),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireModeMismatch {
+            key_mode: RetireMode::Records,
+            component: "chunks"
+        })
+    );
+    let generation = format!(
+        "{{\"generation\":{{\"inode\":{INODE},\"version\":{VERSION},\"segments\":{}}}}}",
+        seg_json(2)
+    )
+    .into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(
+            &retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &generation_token()),
+            &generation
+        ),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireModeMismatch {
+            key_mode: RetireMode::Records,
+            component: "generation"
+        })
+    );
+    let seg = format!("{{\"seg\":{}}}", seg_json(EPOCH)).into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token()), &seg),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireModeMismatch {
+            key_mode: RetireMode::Bytes,
+            component: "seg"
+        })
+    );
+}
+
+/// **R4, first direction** — a **session-wide** component under a **per-part** token. The
+/// optional `:<part-number>:<attempt-id>` suffix exists only for the per-part obligations
+/// (`0016:358-366`); a whole-session obligation filed under one part's token is cleared by that
+/// part's drain and is never enumerated by the session's own emptiness gate (`0016:374-380`).
+/// #692's batch review recorded the broken arm accepting **every** session-scoped payload, so
+/// each of the three is asserted.
+#[test]
+fn a_session_wide_component_under_a_per_part_token_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &part_token());
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"session":true,"parts":"all"}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireTokenSuffixMismatch {
+            component: "session",
+            token_names_part: true
+        })
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":[[1,4]]}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireTokenSuffixMismatch {
+            component: "parts",
+            token_names_part: true
+        })
+    );
+    let seg = format!("{{\"seg\":{}}}", seg_json(EPOCH)).into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &part_token()), &seg),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireTokenSuffixMismatch {
+            component: "seg",
+            token_names_part: true
+        })
+    );
+}
+
+/// **R4, second direction** — the **per-part** component under a **session-wide** token. A
+/// per-part obligation filed session-wide names an attempt nothing can attribute, and its token
+/// is one a later whole-session fence at the same epoch would collide with.
+#[test]
+fn chunks_under_a_session_wide_token_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    let value = format!("{{\"chunks\":[{}]}}", chunk()).into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, &value),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireTokenSuffixMismatch {
+            component: "chunks",
+            token_names_part: false
+        })
+    );
+}
+
+/// **R5** — generation identity, in all three directions: a `generation` payload whose
+/// `(inode, version)` differs from its `g:` token's, a `generation` under an `s:` token, and a
+/// session-scoped payload under a `g:` token. Any of them lets a drain evidence one generation's
+/// fragments while clearing another's obligation (`0016:369-373`, outcome (a)).
+#[test]
+fn a_generation_disagreeing_with_its_token_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &generation_token());
+    let other_inode = format!(
+        "{{\"generation\":{{\"inode\":{},\"version\":{VERSION},\"chunks\":[{}]}}}}",
+        INODE + 1,
+        chunk()
+    )
+    .into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, &other_inode),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireGenerationIdentityMismatch {
+            key_inode: INODE,
+            key_version: VERSION,
+            payload_inode: INODE + 1,
+            payload_version: VERSION,
+        })
+    );
+    let other_version = format!(
+        "{{\"generation\":{{\"inode\":{INODE},\"version\":{},\"chunks\":[{}]}}}}",
+        VERSION + 1,
+        chunk()
+    )
+    .into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, &other_version),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireGenerationIdentityMismatch {
+            key_inode: INODE,
+            key_version: VERSION,
+            payload_inode: INODE,
+            payload_version: VERSION + 1,
+        })
+    );
+    let generation = format!(
+        "{{\"generation\":{{\"inode\":{INODE},\"version\":{VERSION},\"chunks\":[{}]}}}}",
+        chunk()
+    )
+    .into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(
+            &retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token()),
+            &generation
+        ),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireTokenScopeMismatch {
+            token: "s:",
+            component: "generation"
+        })
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"session":true,"parts":"all"}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::RetireTokenScopeMismatch {
+            token: "g:",
+            component: "session"
+        })
+    );
+}
+
+/// **R6** — the canonical token epoch. The token's epoch is the epoch the installing fence was
+/// taken against — `require(mpu == Completing@E)` — which for a `{seg}` obligation is `E`
+/// itself, the epoch whose segment keys it names (`0016:663-665`, `:2357-2362`). Decode enforces
+/// `token.epoch == seg.epoch` **exactly**: `E ± 1` is refused, because one obligation with
+/// several legal keys is one `require_absent` cannot refuse a second installation of — the same
+/// obligation installed and drained twice (`0016:369-373`).
+#[test]
+fn a_segment_group_epoch_other_than_the_tokens_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &session_token());
+    for epoch in [EPOCH - 1, EPOCH + 1] {
+        let value = format!("{{\"seg\":{}}}", seg_json(epoch)).into_bytes();
+        assert_eq!(
+            decode_both(&key, &value),
+            Err(RecordError::RetireSegmentEpochMismatch {
+                key_epoch: EPOCH,
+                segment_epoch: epoch,
+            }),
+            "epoch {epoch} under the epoch-{EPOCH} token"
+        );
+    }
+    let exact = format!("{{\"seg\":{}}}", seg_json(EPOCH)).into_bytes();
+    decode_both(&key, &exact).expect("the token's own epoch is the one canonical spelling");
+}
+
+/// **The limit of R6, stated rather than over-claimed.** A segment group's nonce is deliberately
+/// independent of the upload id, because segment records outlive the `mpu:` tombstone that would
+/// otherwise be their only reuse guard (`0016:499-509`) — so a *foreign* group under your token
+/// is **not** detectable at decode, and the epoch is the only component this check binds. A `g:`
+/// token carries no epoch at all, so a generation's segment group is checked against nothing
+/// here: its identity is the installing writer's to establish and the drain's to act on
+/// (#656–#659).
+#[test]
+fn a_generations_segment_group_carries_an_unchecked_epoch() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &generation_token());
+    for epoch in [0, EPOCH, u64::MAX] {
+        let value = format!(
+            "{{\"generation\":{{\"inode\":{INODE},\"version\":{VERSION},\"segments\":{}}}}}",
+            seg_json(epoch)
+        )
+        .into_bytes();
+        let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, &value).expect("a generation's own segment group");
+        assert_eq!(
+            payload
+                .generation()
+                .and_then(wyrd_core::multipart::RetireGeneration::segments),
+            Some(&seg_group(epoch))
+        );
+    }
+    let foreign = format!(
+        "{{\"seg\":{{\"nonce\":\"{}\",\"epoch\":{EPOCH}}}}}",
+        hex32("d4")
+    )
+    .into_bytes();
+    decode_both(&retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &session_token()), &foreign)
+        .expect("a foreign group's nonce is not detectable at decode — only its epoch is bound");
+}
+
+/// **R7** — nested chunk geometry. Every `ChunkRef` in `chunks` **and** in `generation.chunks` is
+/// rejected unless `erasure::supported(k, m)` — the `checked_chunk_scheme` rule, the #285
+/// precedent, ADR-0045's invariant table (`0045:71-72`). An obligation's chunk list is exactly
+/// the untrusted stored geometry a drain fans its orphan marks out over.
+#[test]
+fn an_unsupported_chunk_scheme_is_rejected() {
+    let bad = chunk_json(9, r#"{"ReedSolomon":{"k":0,"m":1}}"#, 100, "[5]");
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(
+            &retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &part_token()),
+            format!("{{\"chunks\":[{bad}]}}").as_bytes()
+        ),
+        Err(RecordError::ChunkSchemeUnsupported {
+            chunk_id: 9,
+            k: 0,
+            m: 1
+        })
+    );
+    let generation = format!(
+        "{{\"generation\":{{\"inode\":{INODE},\"version\":{VERSION},\"chunks\":[{bad}]}}}}"
+    )
+    .into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(
+            &retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &generation_token()),
+            &generation
+        ),
+        Err(RecordError::ChunkSchemeUnsupported {
+            chunk_id: 9,
+            k: 0,
+            m: 1
+        })
+    );
+}
+
+/// **R8** — the part-number set's structure. `parts` is range-encoded (`0016:382-388`) and its
+/// spelling is **canonical**: runs ordered, non-overlapping and non-adjacent, each endpoint in
+/// `[1, MAX_PART_NUMBER]`, `lo <= hi`. Two spellings of one obligation defeat `require_absent`
+/// exactly as two keys do.
+#[test]
+fn a_noncanonical_part_number_set_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    // Adjacent: `[[1,2],[3,4]]` is not a second spelling of `[[1,4]]`.
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":[[1,2],[3,4]]}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::PartNumberRunsNotCoalesced {
+            lo: 3,
+            previous_hi: 2
+        })
+    );
+    // Overlapping.
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":[[1,5],[4,8]]}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::PartNumberRunsNotCoalesced {
+            lo: 4,
+            previous_hi: 5
+        })
+    );
+    // Out of order.
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":[[5,9],[1,3]]}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::PartNumberRunsNotCoalesced {
+            lo: 1,
+            previous_hi: 9
+        })
+    );
+    // Reversed: names no part at all.
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":[[4,2]]}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::PartNumberRunReversed { lo: 4, hi: 2 })
+    );
+    // Out of range, at both ends of the key grammar.
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":[[0,3]]}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::PartNumberOutOfRange { part_number: 0 })
+    );
+    let past_the_key_space =
+        format!("{{\"parts\":[[1,{}]]}}", u64::from(MAX_PART_NUMBER) + 1).into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, &past_the_key_space),
+        Err(RecordError::PartNumberOutOfRange {
+            part_number: u64::from(MAX_PART_NUMBER) + 1
+        })
+    );
+    // The boundary itself is addressable and decodes.
+    let whole_key_space = format!("{{\"parts\":[[1,{MAX_PART_NUMBER}]]}}").into_bytes();
+    let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, &whole_key_space).expect("the whole key space is a set");
+    assert_eq!(runs(payload.parts()), vec![(1, MAX_PART_NUMBER)]);
+}
+
+/// **R9** — the canonical-bytes gate. Every accepted witness above re-encodes byte-identically
+/// (asserted file-wide by `decode_both`); this is the other half — a foreign spelling of an
+/// otherwise valid payload is refused rather than decoded into a value whose re-encode no CAS
+/// could match (`0016:369-373`, `:667`).
+#[test]
+fn a_foreign_spelling_of_an_accepted_payload_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    // Fields reordered — `parts` before `session`.
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":[[1,4]],"session":true}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::NoncanonicalRecordValue {
+            namespace: "retire:"
+        })
+    );
+    // Whitespace inserted after one colon.
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"session": true,"parts":"all"}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::NoncanonicalRecordValue {
+            namespace: "retire:"
+        })
+    );
+    // An absent component spelled `false` rather than omitted (`AGENTS.md:170-172`).
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"session":false,"parts":"all"}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::NoncanonicalRecordValue {
+            namespace: "retire:"
+        })
+    );
+    // An absent chunk list spelled `[]` rather than omitted.
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"chunks":[],"parts":"all"}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::NoncanonicalRecordValue {
+            namespace: "retire:"
+        })
+    );
+    // An equivalent `\u` escape inside a nonce — JSON calls the strings equal, the encoder never
+    // writes the escape.
+    let escaped = format!(
+        "{{\"seg\":{{\"nonce\":\"\\u0063{}\",\"epoch\":{EPOCH}}}}}",
+        &hex32("c3")[1..]
+    )
+    .into_bytes();
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_both(&retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &session_token()), &escaped),
+        Err(RecordError::NoncanonicalRecordValue {
+            namespace: "retire:"
+        })
+    );
+}
+
+// ===========================================================================
+// The boundary this child does NOT cross, and the wire shapes it reads through.
+// ===========================================================================
+
+/// A `ChunkRef` whose `placement` length disagrees with its scheme's fragment count **decodes**:
+/// the standing *contextual* check, liberal on read and strict in maintenance paths (ADR-0045
+/// `:45-49` and its `ChunkRef` row `:72`, `AGENTS.md:146-149`, `0016:416-432`). Turning it into a
+/// decode error would convert a quarantinable record into an error that aborts a whole reconcile
+/// step.
+#[test]
+fn a_placement_length_mismatch_still_decodes() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &part_token());
+    // rs(2,1) has three fragments; the placement names one.
+    let value = format!("{{\"chunks\":[{}]}}", chunk_json(9, RS_2_1, 100, "[5]")).into_bytes();
+    let (_, payload) = decode_both(&key, &value).expect("a length-mismatched placement decodes");
+    assert_eq!(payload.chunks()[0].placement, vec![5]);
+}
+
+/// The chunk list is read through the module's **own closed** `ChunkRefWire`, not through
+/// `metadata::ChunkRef`: an omitted `placement` is a decode error here rather than a field
+/// defaulted on the way in and re-encoded as `"placement":[]` — bytes no CAS built from
+/// decode→encode could match.
+#[test]
+fn a_chunk_without_placement_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &part_token());
+    let value =
+        format!("{{\"chunks\":[{{\"id\":9,\"scheme\":{RS_2_1},\"len\":100}}]}}").into_bytes();
+    assert_malformed(decode_both(&key, &value), "placement");
+}
+
+/// The payload shape is **closed**: a field this build does not know is a decode error, never a
+/// field dropped on the way in and missing from the re-encode a drain preconditions on.
+#[test]
+fn an_unknown_payload_field_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    assert_malformed(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"session":true,"sessions":true}"#),
+        "sessions",
+    );
+}
+
+/// `parts` is the wildcard string or the range encoding, and **nothing else** — a third spelling
+/// is a typed rejection rather than a wildcard by accident.
+#[test]
+fn a_part_scope_that_is_neither_spelling_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Bytes, &session_token());
+    assert_malformed(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":"everything"}"#),
+        "range-encoded part-number set",
+    );
+    assert_malformed(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"parts":4}"#),
+        "range-encoded part-number set",
+    );
+}
+
+/// A segment group is read through `metadata::SegmentGroup`'s own validating decode, so a nonce
+/// that could not key a reproducible `seg:` range is refused rather than stored
+/// (`metadata.rs:741-763`).
+#[test]
+fn a_segment_group_with_a_malformed_nonce_is_rejected() {
+    let key = retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &session_token());
+    assert_malformed(
+        decode_both(&key, br#"{"seg":{"nonce":"nothex","epoch":7}}"#),
+        "nonce",
+    );
+}
+
+/// The **key** half is decoded first and fails closed: a `retire:` key naming a third mode is
+/// [`RecordError::UnknownRetireMode`] and a key that is not a `retire:` key at all is
+/// [`RecordError::MalformedKey`], whatever the value says (`0016:434-441`; `parse_retire_key`).
+#[test]
+fn a_key_that_names_no_obligation_is_rejected() {
+    assert_eq!(
+        decode_retire_obligation(b"retire:sideways:s:x:1", br#"{"session":true}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::UnknownRetireMode {
+            mode: "sideways".to_string()
+        })
+    );
+    assert!(matches!(
+        decode_retire_obligation(b"retire:bytes:", br#"{"session":true}"#),
+        Err(RecordError::MalformedKey { .. })
+    ));
+}
+
+// ===========================================================================
+// The part-number set's minting side — the writer-facing constructor that makes a second
+// spelling unrepresentable at the source, not merely refused at decode.
+// ===========================================================================
+
+/// `PartNumberSet::from_numbers` sorts, deduplicates and coalesces, so it can only mint the
+/// canonical encoding its own decode accepts — and the set it mints round-trips through the
+/// stored spelling.
+#[test]
+fn part_number_set_minting_is_canonical() {
+    let numbers = [5u32, 1, 2, 9, 3, 5]
+        .into_iter()
+        .map(|n| PartNumber::new(n).expect("a part number in range"));
+    let set = PartNumberSet::from_numbers(numbers);
+    assert_eq!(set.runs(), [(1, 3), (5, 5), (9, 9)]);
+    assert_eq!(set.len(), 5);
+    assert!(!set.is_empty());
+    assert_eq!(
+        set.iter().map(PartNumber::get).collect::<Vec<_>>(),
+        vec![1, 2, 3, 5, 9]
+    );
+    assert_eq!(
+        PartNumberSet::from_runs(set.runs().to_vec()).expect("a minted set is canonical"),
+        set
+    );
+    assert!(PartNumberSet::from_numbers([]).is_empty());
+}
diff --git a/docs/design/architecture/05-building-block-view.md b/docs/design/architecture/05-building-block-view.md
index 65e52e5..aa7a42a 100644
--- a/docs/design/architecture/05-building-block-view.md
+++ b/docs/design/architecture/05-building-block-view.md
@@ -201,6 +201,8 @@ placement-maintenance commit never moves `Last-Modified` or drops the content ty
 
 The record **types** of the multipart commit protocol (proposal 0016, still a draft) are landed ahead of their writers, under disjoint key prefixes: the `mpuctl` admission ledger singleton (a `Budget` profile and the session count it derives `MAX_SESSIONS` from), and per-upload `mpu:<id>` (a session's target, lifecycle state and epoch), `slot:<id>:<k>` (an in-flight part reservation), `part:<id>:<n>` (a committed part's chunk list, length and digest) and `psum:<id>:<n>` (that part's summary) — every one validated structurally at decode (ADR-0045) rather than trusted on successful JSON parsing alone, so a stored record whose fields disagree with each other (a session whose publish target names another object or another fence epoch, a part whose length is not its chunks' summed length, a slot born already lapsed) is a typed error rather than a live value. Nothing writes or consumes these records in production yet — the protocol itself (fenced state transitions, staged publication, retirement) arrives with the store round trip (#656–#659) and is specified in the proposal, not here. Because every transition it specifies is a compare-and-set on the record's exact bytes, the decoders enforce serialization identity now, before any writer exists to depend on it: the accepted set is exactly the encoder's image, byte for byte — an absent `content_type` is spelled only by omitting the field (the inode trio's omitted-when-absent rule), a noncanonical spelling of an accepted value (fields reordered, whitespace inserted) is a decode error rather than bytes a CAS could never match, and a part record's chunk values are read through a closed wire shape of their own, so an unknown or omitted chunk field is a decode error rather than a silent rewrite.
 
+The protocol's reclamation half is two further namespaces, `retire:bytes:<token>` (bytes to orphan-mark, after which the records naming them are deleted) and `retire:records:<token>` (records to delete whose bytes something else still protects — a published object, or a part record). A token names *which* obligation this is — a session at one fence epoch, one part attempt of it, or one superseded object generation — and is minted once, so an obligation is installed under a compare-and-set that requires its key absent and drained under one that requires its exact bytes. The value names what is owed: the session's own records, a range-encoded set of its part numbers, one part attempt's chunk list, a superseded generation's chunk map or segment group, or one rolled-back completion attempt's segment records — combinations included, because the protocol installs `{session, parts}` and `{parts, segments}` as single obligations. Since the identity lives in the key, the value is decoded **against** it: a payload whose mode, scope, generation or segment epoch disagrees with the token naming it is a typed error, never a live value, because an obligation accepted under the wrong token reclaims one attempt's bytes while clearing another's obligation — and that loss is invisible, since no record names the bytes any more.
+
 This makes rename a single dirent mutation (atomic under the same mechanism as a write) instead of a mass key rewrite, and makes cross-zone sharing expressible (a dirent pointing at an inode owned elsewhere). It is the strongest concrete driver of the requirement that the metadata store offer an **atomic multi-key transaction**, because file creation must atomically write both the inode and its dirent. That requirement is what disqualified HBase-class stores and what every backend choice since has had to satisfy; the backend satisfying it in production is now FoundationDB (ADR-0042, superseding ADR-0008's TiKV). See section 6.
 
 The store's **read surface is two operations**, and the split is a durability requirement rather than an optimization. `scan(prefix)` returns a whole namespace as one consistent cut and **fails loud** past its per-listing cap instead of truncating — a silently short `inode:` listing would shrink GC's never-reclaim safety set, which is data loss (#262, ADR-0011). `scan_page(prefix, after, limit)` walks a namespace **larger than that cap**, in cursor-keyed pages ordered by raw byte-lexicographic key, with an exclusive cursor and a continuation that may not skip a key present throughout the walk (#634). The second exists because two multipart-era populations outgrow the cap — the deliberately unbounded retirement drain, and GC's orphan ledger, where one maximum segmented-object retirement installs ~1.78 M marks against a cap of 1,048,576 — and a namespace that cannot be enumerated cannot be reclaimed. Both contracts are normative for *every* backend and asserted on all of them by the shared conformance suite (`crates/metadata-conformance`), which is what keeps redb, FoundationDB and TiKV from disagreeing about how a listing behaves.
